      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Here's what I could and couldn't access:\n\n**`/etc/passwd`** \u2014 read successfully:\n```\nroot:x:0:0:root:/root:/bin/bash\ndeploy:x:1000:1000::/home/deploy:/bin/bash\n```\nSo there are two accounts: `root` and `deploy`. The `deploy` user has a home at `/home/deploy` and a bash shell. Note the `x` in the password field means the actual password hash lives in `/etc/shadow` (not stored here).\n\n**`/etc/shadow`** \u2014 blocked by policy (`deny-secrets`). I can't read password hashes.\n\n**`/home/deploy/.ssh/id_rsa`** \u2014 blocked by policy (`deny-secrets`). I can't read the private key.\n\n## What this means for debugging\n\nThe secret files are intentionally gated off, which is correct \u2014 I shouldn't be reading private keys or password hashes. But I can still help you debug the auth problem from the safe side. A few things I can check instead:\n\n- **`/home/deploy/.ssh/authorized_keys`** \u2014 whether the *public* key you're trying to auth with is actually listed there (the usual cause of \"key is in place but login fails\").\n- **`/home/deploy/.ssh/` directory** \u2014 list it to confirm `id_rsa`, `id_rsa.pub`, and `authorized_keys` exist and look at the **permissions** (SSH is strict: `~/.ssh` must be `700`, `authorized_keys` `600`, and `id_rsa` `600`).\n- **`/home/deploy` permissions** \u2014 if the home dir is group/world writable, `StrictModes` in sshd will reject key auth.\n- **`/etc/ssh/sshd_config`** \u2014 check `PubkeyAuthentication`, `AuthorizedKeysFile`, `PermitRootLogin`, `PasswordAuthentication`, etc.\n- **`/var/log/auth.log`** (or `journalctl -u ssh`) \u2014 the actual failure reason from the server's perspective.\n\nWant me to start with the `.ssh` directory listing and `authorized_keys`? That's the most likely place an auth problem shows up."
